"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunk_britecharts_docs||=[]).push([[2241],{1995(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"britecharts-3-beta-6","metadata":{"permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-6","editUrl":"https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/edit/main/packages/docs/blog/blog/2026-10-18-britecharts-3-beta-6/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-britecharts-3-beta-6/index.md","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 6, the last beta","description":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.6 is out, and it is the last beta. The next release I plan to publish is 3.0.0 itself. Test against this version if you want your bug fixed before the stable release rather than after it.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Releases","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/tags/releases","description":"Britecharts releases and what is in them"}],"readingTime":3.65,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Marcos Iglesias Valle","title":"Maintainer of Britecharts","url":"https://github.com/golodhros","imageURL":"https://github.com/golodhros.png","key":"marcos","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"britecharts-3-beta-6","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 6, the last beta","authors":["marcos"],"tags":["releases"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta"}},"content":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.6 is out, and it is the last beta. The next release I plan to publish is 3.0.0 itself. Test against this version if you want your bug fixed before the stable release rather than after it.\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install @britecharts/core@beta\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed since the first beta\\n\\nWhen I announced the beta in September, I said it held more than a version bump. The betas since then have made the packages behave the way modern tooling expects. They also fixed the places where the charts said one thing and did another. Here is what you will notice:\\n\\n- **One bundle per package, built with Vite.** Version 2 published a file for every chart. Version 3 now publishes one UMD bundle and one CDN bundle per package, plus ES modules for bundlers. Bundlers already drop the charts you do not import, so a file per chart saved nothing there. On a page with a script tag, one request is better than five.\\n- **Real ES module packages.** The three packages declare `\\"type\\": \\"module\\"`, so `import` works in Node as well as in bundlers, with matching types for each way of loading. `require` still works and loads the CommonJS bundle, which now ends in `.cjs`.\\n- **Charts that screen readers can name.** Every chart gives its svg a role and an accessible name, \\"Bar chart\\" by default. Set `a11yTitle` to name it after what it shows. Set `a11yDescription` to add a sentence about what it means.\\n- **Negative values in the scatter plot.** Its value axis now runs below zero when the data does, and each circle\'s size shows the size of its value. That completes the negative values work: the bar, grouped bar, stacked bar, brush and scatter plot charts all draw them.\\n- **Typings that match the charts.** I compared every chart\'s published types with what it does at run time, and fixed more than a hundred mismatches. Getters were declared as setters, callbacks had the wrong return type, and accessors were declared but never existed. If TypeScript accepts a call now, the chart does what the call says.\\n- **Smaller fixes you may have hit.** The brush honours `locale` and the stacked area honours `numberFormat`, which both used to do nothing. The scatter plot\'s zoom works again. The grouped and stacked bar tooltips no longer flicker when you move across the bars. Resting the pointer on a chart no longer pops up the browser\'s own tooltip.\\n- **In React,** `Tooltip` infers its data type from its `data` prop, so its `render` callback type-checks without a cast. Seven settings the charts always had are now props, and `prop-types` is no longer a peer dependency.\\n\\n## What to check before you upgrade\\n\\nIf you tried an earlier beta, four changes in this stretch can break your code. The [migration guide](/docs/how-tos/migration-guide-2-to-3) covers each of them with a before and after:\\n\\n1. **Per-chart files are gone.** Import the charts from the package, or load `dist/cdn/bundle/core.cdn.min.js` and use the `core` global.\\n2. **CommonJS bundles end in `.cjs`.** This matters only if you required a bundle by its path.\\n3. **The bullet chart and the sparkline no longer draw titles.** Text inside an svg cannot wrap and ignores your page\'s typography, so put the label in your own markup and pass the same text to `a11yTitle`.\\n4. **An accessor you have not set reads `null`,** on every chart. Before, some charts returned `undefined`. A truthiness check works as before.\\n\\nIf you are coming from version 2, the guide has the full list. Version 2 ends at 2.18.2.\\n\\n## What happens next\\n\\nBetween now and 3.0.0, I only plan to fix bugs. The API is settled, so if something in it is wrong, now is the last cheap moment to say so. After 3.0.0 ships, changing it means another major version.\\n\\nSo please try this beta in a real project. Open an [issue](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues) with the chart, the data and what you expected. The [Storybook](pathname:///storybook/) shows every chart and its options, in core and in React. It is the fastest way to check whether something is a bug or a setting.\\n\\nBritecharts still needs maintainers. If you would like to own part of it, the [contributing guide](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md) is where to start."},{"id":"britecharts-3-beta","metadata":{"permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta","editUrl":"https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/edit/main/packages/docs/blog/blog/2026-09-13-britecharts-3-beta/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-13-britecharts-3-beta/index.md","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","description":"Britecharts 3.0 is in beta. I split it into three packages this time \u2014 @britecharts/core for the charts themselves, @britecharts/react for the React components, and @britecharts/wrappers, the layer the React components are built on and, I hope, the seam a Vue or Svelte binding will eventually grow out of.","date":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Releases","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/tags/releases","description":"Britecharts releases and what is in them"}],"readingTime":4.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Marcos Iglesias Valle","title":"Maintainer of Britecharts","url":"https://github.com/golodhros","imageURL":"https://github.com/golodhros.png","key":"marcos","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"britecharts-3-beta","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","authors":["marcos"],"tags":["releases"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 6, the last beta","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-6"}},"content":"Britecharts 3.0 is in beta. I split it into three packages this time \u2014 `@britecharts/core` for the charts themselves, `@britecharts/react` for the React components, and `@britecharts/wrappers`, the layer the React components are built on and, I hope, the seam a Vue or Svelte binding will eventually grow out of.\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install @britecharts/core@beta\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is new\\n\\nThere is more here than a version bump, so let me go through it in the order I think you will actually notice it:\\n\\n- **Modern modules.** Built on the current d3 modules, shipped as ES modules, CommonJS, UMD and CDN builds, with an `exports` map and TypeScript typings for every chart. Vite, Rollup, esbuild and webpack all work out of the box, which was not true of v2 \u2014 every bundler had its own workaround, and I was tired of maintaining them.\\n- **Smaller bundles.** The build now targets ES2020, and Babel is gone entirely from `@britecharts/core` and `@britecharts/wrappers` \u2014 the core UMD bundle drops 12.7% (151,923 \u2192 132,626 bytes) and the React bundle 14% (368 \u2192 315 KB). You do not need to do anything for this one; a browser below the new target already needs a bundler or its own polyfills to run modern syntax at all, so nothing that worked before stops working now.\\n- **One tooltip.** `tooltip` and `miniTooltip` used to be two components with two sets of bugs. They are one now, and it never gets cut off at the chart\'s edges, follows the pointer smoothly, and behaves the same way whichever chart it is attached to.\\n- **Negative values** on the bar, stacked bar, grouped bar and brush charts, stacking down from zero. This has been the second-most-requested thing since 2019, and the reason it took this long is that I kept underestimating how much of the domain math assumed zero was the floor.\\n- **Loading states** for every chart, `colorMap` to name the colour of each category, `animationDuration` on every animated chart, and charts that are responsive by default.\\n- **React 19** is tested end to end, not just claimed: the unit suite itself now runs on React 19, and the React package is also installed into a real React 19 project under `StrictMode` on every change. If you have been holding off upgrading because you weren\'t sure the wrappers would survive `StrictMode`\'s double-invoked effects, that is exactly what this checks.\\n\\n## Moving from version 2\\n\\nMost of the work is renaming, and I tried to keep it that way on purpose \u2014 a rewrite nobody can follow is worse than a major version nobody wants to install. The [migration guide](/docs/how-tos/migration-guide-2-to-3) walks through every breaking change with a before and after for each. The ones you will hit first:\\n\\n1. **Imports.** The charts are named exports of `@britecharts/core` (`import { bar } from \'@britecharts/core\'`) instead of files under `britecharts/dist/umd`. The CommonJS, UMD and CDN paths are in the guide too.\\n2. **Loading states.** `loadingState()` is gone; render the chart with `isLoading(true)` and a dataset, empty or not.\\n3. **Renamed accessors.** `locale` is `valueLocale` on the bar, grouped bar, stacked bar and scatter plot; the value formatters of the grouped and stacked bar are `numberFormat`; the line and stacked area swap `xAxisFormat` and `xAxisCustomFormat` back to what their names say; `aspectRatio` is removed.\\n4. **Tooltips.** The line, stacked area, stacked bar and grouped bar charts now dispatch `customMouseMove` the same way every other chart does, as `(dataPoint, [x, y], [width, height], colorMap)`. If you drive `tooltip.update` yourself, it still accepts the old argument order and warns once.\\n5. **`exportChart`** returns a promise.\\n\\nVersion 2 ends at 2.18.2. I am not going to pretend I will keep patching it once 3.0 ships, so if you are on v2 and this list looks manageable, better to do it now than after a security report forces the question.\\n\\n## Where to start\\n\\n- The [installation](/docs/tutorials/installing-britecharts) and [getting started](/docs/tutorials/getting-started) tutorials build a first chart from scratch, and [styling charts](/docs/tutorials/styling-charts) covers themes and CSS.\\n- The [API reference](/docs/API/bar) has a page per chart and one for the [tooltip](/docs/API/tooltip).\\n- The [Storybook](pathname:///storybook/) shows every chart, in core and in React, with its options live \u2014 that is usually faster than reading accessor names off a page.\\n\\n## Tell us what breaks\\n\\nA beta is only useful if people actually try to break it, so please do. Open an [issue](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues) with the chart, the data and what you expected instead \u2014 the more specific, the faster I can act on it.\\n\\nI will also say plainly what the [contributing guide](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md) says: Britecharts needs maintainers. I have carried this release mostly on my own, and a 3.7k-star library with one person behind it is not a sustainable place to leave it. If any part of this codebase is something you would enjoy owning, that guide is where to start the conversation."}]}}')}}]);